  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 1218 PM Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Extended period of very warm to hot temperatures, \nwith Thursday and Friday expected to be the hottest.\n\nCentral NC is expected to be in a pattern of above normal \ntemperatures over the next week. This afternoon through Friday, high \npressure located off the coast will bring southerly flow and warm, \nmoist air to the region. Highs this afternoon should remain in the \nupper 80s, rising to the low/mid 90s on Thursday and Friday. With \nincreasing dew points each day, heat indices should be the highest \non Friday, potentially reaching the low 100s. A weak cold front \nlooks to dissipate over the region on Saturday. This does not appear \nto drop temperatures too much, with temperatures Sunday through mid-\nweek remaining in the upper 80s to low 90s. \n\n\n2) Marginal risk for excessive rain (flash flooding) on Saturday.\n\nThe cold front currently across the Great Lakes and Midwest regions \nwill gradually move toward the Carolinas through the week then begin \nto move across our area on Saturday.  While PoPs (showers/tstms) \nwill begin to increase on Friday as the front gets closer, the \nhighest PoPs and thus best chance for shower/tstms with occasional \nheavy downpours will occur on Saturday, particularly during the \nafternoon and evening.  Day 4 ERO indicates a marginal risk for \nflash flooding across all of central NC given abundant pre-frontal \nmoisture advection and risk for cell training.",
